## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP partitioning with a non-sum objective and reconstruction requirements.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with dp[i][t]
- Precomputing max-min for all subarrays
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxMinSubarrayVal(arr){
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxVal = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let curMax = arr[i], curMin = arr[i];
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      curMax = Math.max(curMax, arr[j]);
      curMin = Math.min(curMin, arr[j]);
      maxVal[i][j] = curMax;
      minVal[i][j] = curMin;
    }
  }
  const val = (l,r)=> maxVal[l][r] - minVal[l][r];
  return val;
}

function bestPartition(arr, k){
  const n = arr.length;
  const val = maxMinSubarrayVal(arr);
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = val(0,i-1);
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] + val(j, i-1);
        if(cand > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; parent[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let blocks = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = parent[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize memory to O(nk)?
- Can you handle ties deterministically for block reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model partitioning with a non-linear block value, a common DP pattern in beginner interviews. Requires prefix sums for O(1) block sums, a clear state definition over prefix length and blocks, and backtracking to output endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitions with exact k blocks
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- State: dp[i][t], parent pointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = S[i]*i;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=t;i<=n;i++) {
      for (let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (S[i]-S[p])*(i-p);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i=n, t=k; const ends=[];
  while (t>0) { const p = par[i][t]; ends.push([p+1, i]); i = p; t--; }
  ends.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], windows: ends};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a trick for monotone optima?
- How would you adapt if windows could overlap or if k is variable?

## Why This Is Asked
Models practical scheduling under capacity with a convex (squared) day cost, exercising 2D DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and days
- Transition constrained by daily capacity D
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Backtracking via parent pointers to recover daily blocks
- Complexity: O(n^2 m) time, O(n m) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSchedule(A, m, D) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let d = 1; d <= m; d++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const sum = pref[i] - pref[j];
        if (sum <= D && dp[j][d - 1] !== INF) {
          const cost = dp[j][d - 1] + sum * sum;
          if (cost < dp[i][d]) {
            dp[i][d] = cost;
            par[i][d] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  if (dp[n][m] === INF) return { cost: -1, blocks: [] };
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, d = m;
  while (d > 0) {
    const j = par[i][d];
    blocks.push([j + 1, i]);
    i = j;
    d--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { cost: dp[n][m], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to near O(n m) if D is small or sums have tighter bounds?
- How would you present the actual day blocks to the user, including start/end indices and sums?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft a compact DP with a weighted subsequence objective, a common class of DP optimization.

## Key Concepts
- 1D/2D DP for subsequences; reconstruction; rolling optimization.
- Transition with weight t.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxWeightedSubsequence(A, k){
  const n = A.length; // 1-indexed in problem
  const dp = Array.from({length:k+1}, ()=> Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[0][i]=0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      dp[t][i] = Math.max(dp[t][i-1], dp[t-1][i-1] + t*A[i-1]);
    }
  }
  // backtrack omitted for brevity
  return {value: dp[k][n]};
}
```

