- How does space optimize with a rolling array?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to generalize DP with an extra resource dimension and reconstruction, a common pattern in grid path problems with constraints. It also probes edge-case handling when i or j is at 0 and when t=0. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for resource usage (K diagonals)
- 3-way transitions and path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(INF)));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0 && t === 0) continue;
        let best = INF, pick = null;
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j][t]; pick = [i-1, j, t]; }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] < best) { best = dp[i][j-1][t]; pick = [i, j-1, t]; }
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i-1][j-1][t-1] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j-1][t-1]; pick = [i-1, j-1, t-1]; }
        if (best < INF) {
          dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
          if (pick) parent[i][j][t] = pick;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // find best end state
  let bestCost = INF, endT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    if (dp[n-1][m-1][t] < bestCost) { bestCost = dp[n-1][m-1][t]; endT = t; }
  }
  // reconstruct path
  const path = [];
  let i = n-1, j = m-1, t = endT;
  while (i !== 0 || j !== 0 || t !== 0) {
    path.push([i, j]);
    const p = parent[i][j][t];
    if (!p) break;
    [i, j, t] = p;
  }
  path.push([0,0]);
  path.reverse();
  return { cost: bestCost, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce space to O(m K) using rolling arrays?
- How would you handle negative costs or unreachable endpoints?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP over partitions with a non-linear block cost dependent on average, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP over k blocks
- Prefix sums for O(1) block sums
- Non-linear, floor(sum/len) cost
- Backtracking for endpoints
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const sum = pref[i]-pref[p];
        const len = i-p;
        const cost = Math.floor(sum/len);
        if(dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [], i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const p = par[i][t];
    blocks.push([p+1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize to faster than O(n^2 k)?
- How to handle negatives or alternative cost such as max/min?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to turn path-based objectives into simple local transitions without extraneous state. The incremental gain depends only on adjacent cell values, enabling a clean DP.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with monotone moves
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Space optimization potential

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxAscents(grid){
  const n=grid.length, m=grid[0].length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(0));
  const par=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(null));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      if(i===0 && j===0) continue;
      let top=-Infinity, left=-Infinity;
      if(i>0) top = dp[i-1][j] + (grid[i-1][j] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(j>0) left = dp[i][j-1] + (grid[i][j-1] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(top >= left){ dp[i][j] = top; par[i][j] = 'U'; } else { dp[i][j] = left; par[i][j] = 'L'; }
    }
  }
  // backtrack to build path
  return {score: dp[n-1][m-1], path: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: parity constraint on subarrays adds a practical twist to classic DP without changing core technique.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Ending a subarray with even length using prefix sums
- Path reconstruction and memory optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEvenSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let l = 2; l <= i; l += 2) {
          const val = dp[i-l][t-1] + (S[i] - S[i-l]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            choice[i][t] = l;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

